      <div class="prose">
        <div class="abstract"><h2>Abstract</h2>
          <p>This is a collaboration from the lab, led by Sang Hoe Kim, in which I am second of seven authors. ConvFactorNet keeps the arithmetic of a linear factor model — returns are factor values times stock-specific betas — but replaces the hand-chosen factors with latent ones learned by a convolutional network that reads the entire cross-section of stocks × features at a single date. Because the factors are never observed, the network is trained through the return identity: predict factors, solve for betas by ridge-regularised least squares inside the batch, reconstruct returns, and backpropagate the return error. Grad-CAM on the last convolutional layer then attributes each factor back to the 110 input features, which turns out to say something specific and stable — macroeconomic variables dominate, technical indicators are last — and that attribution is used as a feature-selection rule that improves the strategy rather than merely explaining it.</p>
          <div class="kw"><b>Keywords</b> deep learning · convolutional neural networks · factor modeling · stock return prediction · explainable AI</div>
        </div>

        <div class="stats" style="margin-top:32px">
          <div class="stat"><div class="v">21.67<small>%/yr</small></div><div class="l">CAGR, long–short, best Sharpe config</div><div class="d">101 monthly rebalances, gross</div></div>
          <div class="stat"><div class="v">3.78</div><div class="l">Sharpe ratio</div><div class="d">vs 0.474 CNN-Direct, 0.167 KOSPI</div></div>
          <div class="stat"><div class="v">54.7<small>%</small></div><div class="l">Best directional accuracy</div><div class="d">macro F1 0.5002 — modest</div></div>
