        <h2 id="b1"><span class="num">1</span>Introduction</h2>
        <h3 id="b1-1"><span class="num">1.1</span>The idea</h3>
        <p>A linear factor model says returns are a small set of common factor values combined through stock-specific loadings. Its two weaknesses are well known: the factors are chosen by a human from theory and precedent, and the mapping from inputs to returns is assumed linear. The usual deep-learning response is to drop the structure and predict returns directly. This paper does the opposite — it keeps the structure and learns only the part that was arbitrary.</p>
        <p>The specific move is where the network is inserted. ConvFactorNet does not take one stock's history and emit that stock's return. It takes the <em>whole cross-section at one date</em> — an <i>n</i> × <i>m</i> matrix of <i>n</i> stocks by <i>m</i> = 110 features — as if it were an image, and emits <i>m</i>′ factor values for that date. Betas remain what they always were: an ordinary least-squares fit of realised returns on factor values over a preceding window. The forecast is then the classical product.</p>
        <h3 id="b1-2"><span class="num">1.2</span>The hypothesis</h3>
        <p>Two claims, and both are tested against an explicit counterfactual rather than against nothing.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>The factor bottleneck helps.</b> Routing the prediction through a low-dimensional factor layer beats a network of the same architecture whose final layer predicts returns directly. The paper builds that counterfactual — <i>CNN-Direct</i> — precisely so the claim can fail.</li>
          <li><b>The explanation is actionable, not decorative.</b> If Grad-CAM genuinely identifies the inputs the factors are built from, then re-training on only the high-attribution inputs should <em>improve</em> performance, not merely leave it unchanged. A flat or worse result would say the attribution is cosmetic.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="b2"><span class="num">2</span>Method</h2>
        <figure>
